<p>There are&nbsp;“Italian, French, English-language, German, Central European, Slavic, Latin American and many other literatures,”&nbsp;including works of “detective fiction and science fiction, popular literature and the <em>feuilleton</em>.” As anyone who’s read <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2016/02/umberto-eco-explains-the-poetic-power-of-charles-schulzs-peanuts.html">Eco’s essay on&nbsp;<em>Peanuts</em></a> might imagine, “his comic book collection is also of great importance.”</p>
<p>Indeed, as any fan would expect, Eco’s namesake library reflects not just the vast sweep of his humanistic interests and knowledge, but also his&nbsp;playful sense of eccentricity, and even grotesquerie. According to the <a href="https://fondazioneumbertoeco.org/en/lebiblioteche">Fondazione Umberto Eco</a>, most of the volumes it contains “are marked by a distinctive <em>ex libris</em>,” an adaptation of a sixteenth-century woodcut that depicts a donkey on a cornucopia-topped latrine. “The animal plays a small pipe, setting in motion a merry dance of a dozen cavorting monkeys.” The books themselves have been painstakingly arranged just as Eco shelved them, in accordance with the “good neighbor” principle of placing seemingly unrelated titles in close proximity in order to reveal unexpected intellectual connections between them. The volumes from Eco’s country house in Monte Cerignone will also be incorporated into the collection, but as the man himself knew, the best libraries, like the best minds, are never truly complete.</p>
